      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the remainder when the sum \\(1 + 7 + 13 + 19 + \\cdots + 253 + 259\\) is divided by 6, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the type of sequence:**\n   The given sequence is an arithmetic sequence where the first term \\(a = 1\\) and the common difference \\(d = 6\\). The general form of the \\(n\\)-th term of an arithmetic sequence is given by:\n   \\[\n   a_n = a + (n-1)d\n   \\]\n   We need to find the number of terms \\(n\\) in the sequence. The last term is 259. Setting up the equation for the \\(n\\)-th term:\n   \\[\n   259 = 1 + (n-1) \\cdot 6\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\(n\\):\n   \\[\n   259 = 1 + 6(n-1) \\implies 258 = 6(n-1) \\implies n-1 = 43 \\implies n = 44\n   \\]\n   So, there are 44 terms in the sequence.\n\n2. **Sum of the arithmetic sequence:**\n   The sum \\(S_n\\) of the first \\(n\\) terms of an arithmetic sequence is given by:\n   \\[\n   S_n = \\frac{n}{2} (a + l)\n   \\]\n   where \\(l\\) is the last term. Substituting the known values:\n   \\[\n   S_{44} = \\frac{44}{2} (1 + 259) = 22 \\cdot 260 = 5720\n   \\]\n\n3. **Find the remainder when the sum is divided by 6:**\n   To find \\(5720 \\mod 6\\), we use the property of modular arithmetic. We can simplify the calculation by reducing each term modulo 6 and then summing these values:\n   \\[\n   1 \\mod 6 = 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   7 \\mod 6 = 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   13 \\mod 6 = 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   19 \\mod 6 = 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\vdots\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   259 \\mod 6 = 1\n   \\]\n   There are 44 terms, and each term is congruent to 1 modulo 6. Therefore, the sum of these terms is:\n   \\[\n   5720 \\equiv 44 \\cdot 1 \\equiv 44 \\equiv 2 \\mod 6\n   \\]\n   So, the remainder when the sum is divided by 6 is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{2}\n   \\]",
