      "text": "To find the remainder when the sum \\(1 + 7 + 13 + 19 + \\cdots + 253 + 259\\) is divided by 6, we first need to determine the number of terms in the sequence and then find the sum of the sequence modulo 6.\n\nThe given sequence is an arithmetic sequence where the first term \\(a = 1\\) and the common difference \\(d = 6\\). The \\(n\\)-th term of an arithmetic sequence is given by:\n\\[a_n = a + (n-1)d.\\]\nWe need to find the number of terms \\(n\\) such that the last term \\(a_n = 259\\). So, we set up the equation:\n\\[259 = 1 + (n-1) \\cdot 6.\\]\nSolving for \\(n\\):\n\\[259 = 1 + 6(n-1),\\]\n\\[259 = 1 + 6n - 6,\\]\n\\[259 = 6n - 5,\\]\n\\[264 = 6n,\\]\n\\[n = 44.\\]\nSo, there are 44 terms in the sequence.\n\nNext, we find the sum of the sequence. The sum \\(S_n\\) of the first \\(n\\) terms of an arithmetic sequence is given by:\n\\[S_n = \\frac{n}{2} (a + l),\\]\nwhere \\(l\\) is the last term. Here, \\(n = 44\\), \\(a = 1\\), and \\(l = 259\\). So, the sum is:\n\\[S_{44} = \\frac{44}{2} (1 + 259) = 22 \\cdot 260 = 5720.\\]\nNow, we need to find the remainder when 5720 is divided by 6. We do this by finding the remainder when 5720 is divided by 6:\n\\[5720 \\div 6 = 953 \\text{ remainder } 2.\\]\nSo, the remainder is 2. Therefore, the remainder when the sum \\(1 + 7 + 13 + 19 + \\cdots + 253 + 259\\) is divided by 6 is:\n\\[\\boxed{2}.\\]",
